# Line endings — one repo, more than one operating system, one answer.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Without it, any tool that writes a file on Windows silently
# converts LF to CRLF, and the next commit rewrites EVERY line of that file. It
# happened on 2026-09-18: a forty-line edit to STATUS.md produced a 2438-line
# diff, and a 1435-line change looked like 3107. A diff nobody can read is a
# review nobody can do.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` means: git decides what is text, stores it as LF in the
# repository, and checks it out as LF everywhere — including Windows, which every
# editor and tool in this project's chain handles (Node, Expo, tsc, VS Code).

* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF. A CRLF shebang line is not executable — the kernel
# looks for `/bin/bash` and finds `/bin/bash\r`.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Binary: never normalised, never diffed as text. `text=auto` detects these
# anyway; naming the formats this repo actually carries makes it explicit rather
# than dependent on heuristics.
*.mp3  binary
*.mp4  binary
*.webm binary
*.webp binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.pdf  binary

# STATUS-LOG.md is an append-at-top log: every PR inserts an entry at the same spot, so two open
# PRs always conflict there. `merge=union` keeps BOTH sides' lines instead of stopping — safe for a
# file where nobody edits old entries. STATUS.md (the board) is deliberately NOT listed: two people
# changing the same board row is a real disagreement and should conflict.
STATUS-LOG.md merge=union
